4.3  From write() to the disk: the block layer

TL;DR
  - A write() goes into the page cache and returns. Later, the filesystem turns
    dirty pages into block requests ("write these sectors").
  - The block layer merges neighbouring requests, orders them through an I/O
    scheduler, and hands them to the driver. The device signals each completion
    with an interrupt.
  - Each disk has its scheduler: none for NVMe, which sorts nothing itself;
    mq-deadline or bfq for slower disks.

      cat /sys/block/nvme0n1/queue/scheduler     # the active one in [brackets]
      lsblk -t                                   # block sizes, scheduler

On your machine
  nvme0n1   Samsung SSD 980 PRO 500GB: NVMe flash, 476.9 GiB · scheduler none ·
            8 hardware queues · volatile write cache · TRIM
  sda       USB DISK 3.0: USB drive, 30.0 GiB · scheduler mq-deadline · no
            volatile write cache

Pitfalls
  - bfq keeps a desktop responsive while something copies in the background, at
    some CPU cost; on fast NVMe, none is usually best.
  - Changing the scheduler with echo lasts until reboot; a udev rule makes it
    permanent (13.6).
  - O_DIRECT skips the page cache, for databases that cache by themselves. It
    needs aligned buffers, and it isn't a durability promise: only fsync is
    (4.5).

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 4.3 --why

Related
  Commands  cat, lsblk
  Paths     /sys/block/DEV/queue/scheduler
  Hub       atfq disk Disk
  See also  atfq 17.4 The page cache
  See also  atfq 4.5 fsync: when is data really on disk?
